Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best reflects the agreement. 

Merger Agreement Excerpt:

“Material Adverse Effect” means, with respect to the Company, any change, event, or occurrence, whether occurring in isolation or in combination with others, that (a) would reasonably be expected to delay, impair, or prevent the Company from consummating the transactions contemplated by this Agreement, or (b) would have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, operations, or financial condition of the Company. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for the purposes of clause (b) only, none of the following shall be deemed to constitute or be taken into account in determining a Material Adverse Effect: 
(i) general fluctuations in economic indicators or market conditions; 
(ii) changes in foreign exchange or interest rates, or modifications in regulatory frameworks generally applicable to the industry; 
(iii) operational delays or minor disruptions that do not result in a disproportionate negative impact relative to industry peers; 
(iv) force majeure events (including, but not limited to, natural disasters, pandemics, or acts of terrorism), unless such events uniquely and disproportionately affect the Company; 
(v) effects arising from temporary interim measures adopted in response to industry-wide regulatory changes.
Furthermore, with respect to clause (a) pertaining to the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions, the carveouts listed in (i) through (v) shall apply provided that the delay attributable to such events does not exceed a cumulative threshold of 90 days. 

Question: Based on the above excerpt, is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to the Material Adverse Effect carveouts set forth in clauses (i) through (v)? Options:
A: No
B: Yes
Choices:
